# seven_lakes_basin_rim: the Seven Lakes Basin from the rim, hand-drawn
# (BUILD_PLAN 4.3, S5 brief B9; GAME_DESIGN 4.3, 11.7). 160x168.
#
# We stand at the head of the stone staircase on the rim (node
# seven_lakes_basin, 4,900 ft), looking north-northeast down into the
# basin: a bowl of pale glacier-scoured rock and heather benches sprinkled
# with more lakes than you can count at once, Lunch Lake the biggest, below
# us and a little right. On the left the bowl's wall falls from its crest
# to the floor, forest broken by outcrops and cut by gullies; on the right
# Bogachiel Peak's north face rises behind the floor's far edge, in shadow,
# its couloirs leaning together to their scree, old snow lying broken in
# them, one buttress lit, a cumulus standing over its summit. Through the
# bowl's open far end, the peaks across the Sol Duc valley in haze.
# Olympus is behind us. The staircase's rough stones drop from our feet
# over the lip and out of sight; the junction's sign stands on the right.
# No people but the hiker the composer adds at the trail spot, no legible
# words on the sign, and not exactly seven lakes.
#
# How it reads as a bowl you look down into: the rim we stand on is the
# darkest and biggest band, and its top edge, the lip, runs across the
# whole frame, a band of shade under it and blocks of rock standing on it
# against the floor beyond. Directly above the lip, with nothing between,
# lies the floor, already far below: benches of pale rock in tiers that
# step down toward us, each a glacier-blue top over a slate riser, with
# heather between them and tarns set into them, the riser a tarn's far
# bank; its firs clumps of spires a few rows tall; going to glacier-blue
# haze at its far end. The eye jumps from the dark lip to the far floor,
# and that jump is the drop. The walls rise from the floor on both sides,
# darker than it: the left wall in forest, Bogachiel's face in slate.
#
# Every band's top edge stands apart from what is behind it in value at
# every hour, not just in hue: the left wall, the far ridge and the feet
# of the far peaks stand in the horizon's snow band (pink at dusk, glacier
# blue at blue hour, slate at night); the peaks' tops stand against the
# open sky in slate and night navy; and Bogachiel rises above it all with
# a lit edge along its crest. At dusk the floor's rock goes slate and its
# heather forest green, so it never glows like a lit lawn.
#
# Day colors; dusk, blue hour and night are palette remaps. Lit faces are
# on the right, as the stamps' are. Each shape is drawn the AGI way: its
# outline, then a fill from a seed inside; the small ones go row by row,
# so no lone pixel reads as a speck or an eye.
@ sky
# A light day sky: a thin slate top, glacier blue for most of it, and the
# horizon's snow band low behind the far peaks and the left wall. Each
# seam is two rows of checker, a dither in a small dose; the band's seam
# steps a row up and down, and it never runs on as one dotted line across
# the frame: the left wall's crest rises through it at the frame's edge,
# the two far peaks through its middle, and Bogachiel at the right. Each
# band is cut by a line that its fill then takes too: ink rows at the
# top, and for the band's seam a stepped ink line over a stepped
# night-navy one, so each takes its own fill. The sky stops at row 96,
# behind the basin's floor.
