# The mountains beyond the bowl's open end, across the Sol Duc valley,
# in haze: a far ridge in glacier blue (3), low in the snow band, showing
# only between the nearer peaks and Bogachiel; and in front of it two
# peaks in slate (2), each with its east face in night-navy (1) shade,
# rising out of the band and through its seam into the open sky, so the
# seam is broken where they cross it (never one dotted line across the
# frame). Above the band each peak keeps old snow (4) on its lit face, and
# its shaded face's arete catches the light in a line of snow, as
# Olympus's do, so neither stands on the open sky in slate or navy alone
# (at night those are ink against night navy, too close to tell apart;
# snow goes to slate). The peaks come down behind the left wall's crest
# and, in a low slate foot, behind the floor's far edge, short of
# Bogachiel's ridge, so the face's edge stands against the far ridge and
# the band and never against the peaks' slate, and the floor's haze never
# meets the far ridge's glacier blue.
